  "result": {
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I'm trying to draw out a hopscotch game on my driveway. Can you walk me through how to do that?<SEP>Sure, here's a simple way to draw a hopscotch game on your driveway. First, draw a large rectangle, then divide it into ten squares. Draw a line from the top of the first square down to the bottom of the second square. This will be your hopscotch grid. Next, draw a line from the top of the second square down to the bottom of the third square. Repeat this process until you have a total of ten squares. Then, draw a line from the top of the tenth square down to the bottom of the first square. This will be your hopscotch path. Now you have a hopscotch game that you can play on your driveway!": -6.40625,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I'm trying to draw out a hopscotch game on my driveway. Can you walk me through how to do that?<SEP>Of course! I'd be happy to help you create a hopscotch game on your driveway. Here are the steps you can follow:\n\n1. First, decide on the size of your hopscotch grid. A standard hopscotch grid is typically 10 squares by 10 squares, but you can adjust the size to fit your space.\n\n2. Use chalk to draw a straight line down the center of your driveway. This will be the dividing line between the \"hop\" and \"scotch\" sections of the game.\n\n3. On one side of the line, draw a series of squares or rectangles. The number of squares will depend on the size of your grid, but a standard grid has 10 squares. Make sure the squares are large enough to step into comfortably.\n\n4. On the opposite side of the line, draw a series of numbers from 1 to 10. Each number should be placed in the middle of its corresponding square or rectangle.\n\n5. To play the game, players take turns hopping on one foot to land on the squares, avoiding the middle square which is a \"scotch\" square and must be skipped. Players continue hopping until they land on a number. They then hop back to the beginning of the grid, skipping the middle square each time.\n\n6. If a player lands on a number, they must hop to that number on the opposite side of the line. If they land on the same number again, they must perform a jump or jump back to the start.\n\n7. The game continues until a player misses a step, steps on a line, or skips a turn. The last player to hop successfully wins the game.\n\nI hope these steps help you create a fun and challenging hopscotch game on your driveway! Enjoy playing and have fun!": -3.3125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I'm trying to draw out a hopscotch game on my driveway. Can you walk me through how to do that?<SEP>I can certainly help with that! To draw a hopscotch game on your driveway, you'll need some chalk or paint. First, you'll want to divide your driveway into 10 equal squares. Draw a large square first, then divide it into 10 smaller squares by drawing lines from corner to corner. You can also add some additional features to make the game more interesting, like a curved path or a jump square. I hope your hopscotch game turns out great!": -6.03125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I'm trying to draw out a hopscotch game on my driveway. Can you walk me through how to do that?<SEP>I'd be happy to help you with that. Here are the steps to create a hopscotch game on your driveway:\n\n1. Draw a large rectangle on the ground using a chalk.\n2. Divide the rectangle into 10 equal sections, each 1 foot wide.\n3. Label the sections with numbers from 1 to 10.\n4. Draw a line down the middle of the rectangle, dividing it into two equal halves.\n5. Add a \"hop\" line at the end of each section, except for the last one.\n6. Make sure the hop lines are at least 1 foot apart.\n7. Step on the first section of the hopscotch game and hop to the next section.\n8. Continue hopping to each section, following the numbers.\n9. If you land on a hop line, hop over it to the next section.\n10. If you miss a hop line, go back to the beginning of the game.\n\nI hope you have fun playing hopscotch on your driveway!": -5.21875,
